    "https://vacancies.maastrichtuniversity.nl/job/Maastricht-Junior-Researcher-Integrated-Care-and-Osteoarthritis/1375673957/": {
      "title": "Junior Researcher Integrated Care and Osteoarthritis",
      "description": "Junior Researcher Integrated Care and Osteoarthritis Job Details | Maastricht University Skip to main content Language English Nederlands View Profile Language English Nederlands View Profile All jobs Working at Maastricht University Language English Nederlands View Profile Search by Keyword Show More Options Loading... Faculty/Service Center All Job Type All Location All FTE min All FTE max All Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Create Alert × Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Apply now » Junior Researcher Integrated Care and Osteoarthritis Welcome to Maastricht University! How can we make osteoarthritis care more accessible, integrated and sustainable? And how can we bring the right care closer to people’s everyday lives? As a Junior Researcher, you will contribute to the European EUcare4OA project and help develop, implement and evaluate new approaches to osteoarthritis care across Europe. You will become part of the multidisciplinary team of Beweeghuis Maastricht-Heuvelland, working at the intersection of research, healthcare and community-based care. Together with colleagues and international partners, you will help shape the future of integrated osteoarthritis care . Junior Researcher Integrated Care and Osteoarthritis Our goal: The EUcare4OA (Community-Driven, Accessible, and Resilient Ecosystem for Osteoarthritis Care) project is a European research initiative focused on transforming osteoarthritis (OA) care across Europe. Partners from the Netherlands, Belgium and Sweden work together to develop, implement and evaluate an integrated, community-based care model. The aim is to help people with osteoarthritis receive the right care, at the right place, at the right time. EUcare4OA builds on the successful Beweeghuis Maastricht-Heuvelland model. The project aims to strengthen primary and community care by bringing together physiotherapy, patient self-management, community-based support and digital health solutions in one scalable care pathway. As a Junior Researcher, you will contribute to several work packages within the project and help translate research into practice. Your colleagues: You will join the multidisciplinary team of Beweeghuis Maastricht-Heuvelland, embedded within the Department of Orthopedic Surgery at Maastricht University Medical Center+ (MUMC+). You will work at the interface of Orthopedic Surgery and Beweeghuis. Your colleagues include orthopedic surgeons, physiotherapists, researchers, general practitioners, exercise coaches, policymakers and patient representatives. You will also work closely with international project partners from the Netherlands, Belgium and Sweden. Together, we are developing stepped-care pathways that aim to improve the accessibility, quality and sustainability of osteoarthritis care. What you do As a Junior Researcher, you will contribute to research activities across several EUcare4OA work packages. Your work will combine research, collaboration and the practical implementation and evaluation of integrated care . Your activities include: Supporting comparative analyses of osteoarthritis care models in the participating countries. Collecting, managing and analysing quantitative and qualitative research data. Conducting literature reviews and contributing to evidence synthesis. Supporting stakeholder interviews, focus groups and co-creation workshops with patients, healthcare professionals, policymakers and community partners. Supporting the development and evaluation of integrated osteoarthritis care pathways. Assisting with pilot implementation and data collection within regional care networks. Contributing to project reports, scientific publications, presentations and dissemination activities. Collaborating with international project partners from the Netherlands, Belgium and Sweden. Are you ready to set the course for the years ahead? Then we’d love to meet you. What you bring We’re not looking for checkboxes; we’re interested in who you are and what you bring. Do you recognize yourself in this? You: Have a Master’s degree and/or a PHD in Health Sciences, Human Movement Sciences, Public Health, Epidemiology, Physiotherapy, Biomedical Sciences, Healthcare Policy or a related field. Have an affinity with healthcare research and innovation , and an interest in integrated care, implementation science and community-based healthcare. Have research skills, including experience with literature reviews, evidence synthesis and quantitative and/or qualitative research methods. Experience with data analysis software is an asset. Have strong analytical, organisational and problem-solving skills and are able to manage multiple tasks while working independently. Have excellent communication in Dutch (C1 level) and English. You enjoy working with researchers, healthcare professionals, patients and international project partners in a multidisciplinary environment . What we offer At Maastricht University, you’ll work in an international, open, and engaged environment. We offer: A 12-month contract (0,8 fte) with the prospect of a permanent contract based on mutual satisfaction. A gross monthly salary between €3706,00 and €5760,00 (based on full-time employment of 38 hours per week). 8% holiday allowance and an 8.3% year-end bonus. 29 vacation days (based on full-time), four additional days off (Carnival Monday and Tuesday, Good Friday, and Liberation Day), and the possibility to accrue up to 12 extra days through compensation hours. Flexible working hours, a home office allowance, and the option to work from home. Freedom and space to shape your work independently and develop your ideas. A close-knit community of colleagues to collaborate and grow with. A solid pension plan via ABP, company fitness schemes, and access to various university sports facilities. An inspiring work environment in the heart of Europe. For candidates who demonstrate strong performance and motivation, there maybe an opportunity to further develop the research into a PhD trajectory after the initial one-year appointment. About the Faculty of Health, Medicine and Life Sciences (FHML) FHML is committed to health in the broadest sense: from molecule to human, and from healthcare to prevention. We train healthcare professionals and researchers through innovative educational programmes and conduct groundbreaking research in health and well-being. As part of Maastricht UMC+ (MUMC+), our international and interdisciplinary community forms a unique collaboration between university and academic hospital, where education, research, and care come together. Interested? Want to know more about this position or what it’s like to work at our university? Reach out to dr. Thomay Hoelen at thomay.hoelen@mumc.nl. The end date of the publication is November 8, 2026. Apply now via the button below. We look forward to getting to know you! About Maastricht University At Maastricht University, we collaboratively seek solutions to help move the world forward. We do this with 23,300 students and 5,400 employees across 5 regional locations, 6 faculties, and more than 70 research institutes. We encourage you to push boundaries and discover new opportunities for yourself and the world around you. Together, we can find the answers for tomorrow. The vacancy is open for internal and external candidates. In case of equal qualifications, internal candidates will be prioritized. At Maastricht University, we prefer to contact potential candidates directly. We therefore kindly ask that no agencies or intermediaries submit offers or approaches. Maastricht University is committed to promoting and nurturing a diverse and inclusive community. We believe that diversity in our staff and student population contributes to the quality of research and education at UM, and strive to enable this through inclusive policies and innovative projects led by teams of staff and students. We encourage you to apply for this position. Job Type: Academic Faculty/Service Center: Faculty of Health, Medicine and Life Sciences Closing date: 8-11-2026 FTE min: 0,8 FTE max: 0,8 Salary min: €3706,00 Salary max: €5760,00 ID job: 3879 Job Type: Academic Faculty/Service Center: Faculty of Health, Medicine and Life Sciences Closing date: 8-11-2026 FTE min: 0,8 FTE max: 0,8 Salary min: €3706,00 Salary max: €5760,00 ID job: 3879 Apply now » Find similar jobs: Vacatures, All jobs Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab."
    },
    "https://vacancies.maastrichtuniversity.nl/job/Maastricht-Assistant-Professor-in-Methodology-and-Statistics/1375670857/": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Methodology and Statistics",
      "description": "Assistant Professor in Methodology and Statistics Job Details | Maastricht University Skip to main content Language English Nederlands View Profile Language English Nederlands View Profile All jobs Working at Maastricht University Language English Nederlands View Profile Search by Keyword Show More Options Loading... Faculty/Service Center All Job Type All Location All FTE min All FTE max All Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Create Alert × Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Apply now » Assistant Professor in Methodology and Statistics Welcome to Maastricht University! Do you enjoy developing methodological and statistical approaches to helping researchers answer complex scientific questions? As Assistant Professor in Methodology and Statistics, you contribute to high-quality research and education while advancing methodological innovation within the Faculty of Psychology and Neuroscience. In this role, you help strengthen the scientific foundations that enable impactful research and evidence-based education. Assistant Professor in Methodology and Statistics Our goal: To support high-quality research and education within the Faculty of Psychology and Neuroscience through methodological innovation, statistical expertise and interdisciplinary collaboration. Your colleagues: You will join an engaged and collaborative team of researchers and teachers who share a passion for methodology, statistics and scientific quality. Together, you contribute to methodological innovation in an interdisciplinary environment  and you support students and researchers across the faculty. The department works closely with colleagues from the Department of Methodology and Statistics within the Faculty of Health, Medicine and Life Sciences, creating a vibrant academic network with expertise across a broad range of methodological and statistical domains. These include multilevel and mixed models, structural equation modelling, item response theory, intensive longitudinal data analysis, missing data methods, clustering techniques, reliability and agreement, and optimal design. As Assistant Professor, you combine research, teaching and consultancy activities in a role that contributes directly to the faculty’s educational quality and research excellence. You inspire students, support researchers and help shape methodological innovation within the faculty and beyond. What you do You contribute to excellent education and innovative research while strengthening methodological expertise across the faculty. Inspire and support psychology students in developing the methodological and statistical skills needed for future scientific and professional practice. Teach and coordinate statistics and methodology courses within the Bachelor's programme in Psychology and, over time, potentially contribute to advanced Master's-level courses. Develop, implement and evaluate educational activities and teaching materials that promote active and engaging learning. Conduct independent and collaborative research in methodology and statistics, preferably in areas such as Bayesian inference, causal inference, network analysis, structural equation modelling or multilevel modelling. Provide methodological and statistical support to students, researchers and staff within the faculty. Collaborate with interdisciplinary research teams across faculties and research institutes. Contribute to methodological innovation that strengthens research quality throughout the university. Are you ready to help shape the future of research and education? Then we'd love to meet you. What you bring It's not about ticking boxes; it's about your curiosity, expertise and motivation. Do you recognise yourself in this profile? Within our department, collaboration, curiosity and scientific integrity are central. We are looking for someone who enjoys working with others, takes initiative and is motivated to strengthen both education and research. Academic expertise in methodology and statistics – You hold a PhD in Statistics, Psychometrics, Biometrics, Econometrics or a related field and actively contribute to the scientific community through publications in international peer-reviewed journals. Passion for teaching and learning – You have experience teaching statistics or methodology within the behavioural, social or medical sciences and are motivated to contribute to innovative and student-centred education. Strong analytical and programming skills – You have excellent quantitative and programming skills, particularly in R. Experience with JASP is considered an advantage. Collaborative and proactive – You enjoy working independently while actively contributing to interdisciplinary teams. You take initiative, think analytically and communicate complex ideas in a clear and structured way. Strong communicator – You communicate effectively in English, both written and spoken, and are willing to learn Dutch if you are not yet fluent. What we offer At Maastricht University, you’ll work in an international, open, and engaged environment. We offer: A 12-month contract (1,0 fte) with the prospect of a permanent contract based on mutual satisfaction. A gross monthly salary between €4925,00 and €6682,00 (based on full-time employment of 38 hours per week). 8% holiday allowance and an 8.3% year-end bonus. 29 vacation days (based on full-time), four additional days off (Carnival Monday and Tuesday, Good Friday, and Liberation Day), and the possibility to accrue up to 12 extra days through compensation hours. Flexible working hours, a home office allowance, and the option to work from home. Freedom and space to shape your work independently and develop your ideas. A close-knit community of colleagues to collaborate and grow with. A solid pension plan via ABP, company fitness schemes, and access to various university sports facilities. An inspiring work environment in the heart of Europe. About the Faculty of Psychology and Neuroscience (FPN) At FPN, we share a curiosity about the human brain and behaviour. Together with our students, we explore how our brains function and learn why we do what we do. We connect perspectives and contribute to living together in society. Interested? Want to know more about this position or what it’s like to work at our university? Reach out to Prof. dr. Sophie Vanbelle via sophie.vanbelle@maastrichtuniversity.nl. The end date of the publication is 22 November 2026. Interviews will be in week 49. Required documents: CV Motivation letter Two references Apply now via the button below. We look forward to getting to know you! About Maastricht University At Maastricht University, we collaboratively seek solutions to help move the world forward. We do this with 23,300 students and 5,400 employees across 5 regional locations, 6 faculties, and more than 70 research institutes. We encourage you to push boundaries and discover new opportunities for yourself and the world around you. Together, we can find the answers for tomorrow. The vacancy is open for internal and external candidates. In case of equal qualifications, internal candidates will be prioritized. At Maastricht University, we prefer to contact potential candidates directly. We therefore kindly ask that no agencies or intermediaries submit offers or approaches. Maastricht University is committed to promoting and nurturing a diverse and inclusive community. We believe that diversity in our staff and student population contributes to the quality of research and education at UM, and strive to enable this through inclusive policies and innovative projects led by teams of staff and students. We encourage you to apply for this position. Job Type: Academic Faculty/Service Center: Faculty of Psychology and Neuroscience Closing date: 22-11-2026 FTE min: 1,0 FTE max: 1,0 Salary min: €4925,00 Salary max: €6682,00 ID job: 3790 Job Type: Academic Faculty/Service Center: Faculty of Psychology and Neuroscience Closing date: 22-11-2026 FTE min: 1,0 FTE max: 1,0 Salary min: €4925,00 Salary max: €6682,00 ID job: 3790 Apply now » Find similar jobs: Vacatures, All jobs Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab."
    },
    "https://vacancies.maastrichtuniversity.nl/job/Maastricht-Postdoctoral-Researcher-in-Engineered-Living-Materials-and-Programmable-Hydrogels/1375589557/": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher in Engineered Living Materials and Programmable Hydrogels",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Researcher in Engineered Living Materials and Programmable Hydrogels Job Details | Maastricht University Skip to main content Language English Nederlands View Profile Language English Nederlands View Profile All jobs Working at Maastricht University Language English Nederlands View Profile Search by Keyword Show More Options Loading... Faculty/Service Center All Job Type All Location All FTE min All FTE max All Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Create Alert × Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Apply now » Postdoctoral Researcher in Engineered Living Materials and Programmable Hydrogels Welcome to Maastricht University! Join the ERC Consolidator project SupraValent to grow a new research line in engineered living materials. You will combine synthetic biology and microbial engineering with dynamic hydrogels, developing cellular systems that can produce, activate, or reorganize material components in response to defined signals. Postdoctoral Researcher in Engineered Living Materials and Programmable Hydrogels Engineer living cells that sense their environment and actively modify the material around them Our goal: To create a programmable two-way interface between living cells and synthetic hydrogels: the material supports and regulates the cells, while engineered cells modify the material’s structure, mechanics, or biological function over time. Your colleagues: You will join the BioMatt group of Dr. Matthew Baker (https://merlninstitute.com/discover-merln/departments-and-groups/research-groups/biomatt-polymeric-biomaterials). The BioMatt group is part of MERLN’s Department of Instructive Biomaterials Engineering (IBE), which develops biomaterials that actively interact with and guide biological systems. Within this environment, BioMatt combines molecular and polymer design with cell biology and tissue engineering to create dynamic materials for biomedical applications. Working at MERLN means joining an international network of researchers from approximately 25 nationalities and a collaborative community spanning biomaterials, chemistry, mechanobiology, tissue engineering, biofabrication, microbiology, and cell biology. You will work in newly renovated laboratories with access to state-of-the-art facilities and close scientific support from colleagues across the SupraValent team. What you do This postdoctoral position leads the engineered-cell and living-materials work package within SupraValent. You will have substantial freedom to refine the biological strategy while working with existing dynamic hydrogel platforms and reactive chemistries developed by the team. You will: Design, construct, and validate engineered microbial cells that produce, secrete, display, or activate molecules capable of modifying a surrounding hydrogel. Develop inducible or environmentally responsive cellular programs that provide temporal control over material reinforcement, remodeling, degradation, or biofunctionalization. Establish reproducible encapsulation and culture conditions that preserve cellular activity while maintaining useful hydrogel properties and appropriate containment. Quantify the complete living-material feedback loop by connecting gene expression and cellular output to chemical conversion, network structure, rheology, transport, and material function. Identify failure modes at the biology–materials interface, including diffusion limitations, loss of activity, heterogeneous modification, burden on engineered cells, and changes in material integrity. Collaborate with synthetic chemists, polymer scientists, and bioengineers within MERLN and the wider network. Lead publications and conference contributions, contribute to project strategy and reporting, and support the supervision of PhD, MSc, and BSc researchers. The initial objective is a well-controlled proof of concept showing that an engineered cellular output produces a measurable and programmable change in a SupraValent (supramolecular) hydrogel. The longer-term ambition is to develop adaptive materials in which biological and synthetic components cooperate to generate functions that neither component can achieve alone. Are you ready to build materials that are not only compatible with life, but shaped by it? Then we would love to meet you. Scientific background Engineered living materials combine genetically programmable cells with material structures. They can potentially sense signals, perform biochemical transformations, and adapt over time, but a central challenge is to couple cellular activity to a controlled and measurable change in the host material. Passive encapsulation alone does not establish this feedback: the biological output, reaction chemistry, network architecture, and macroscopic material response must be connected. SupraValent provides a distinctive platform for addressing this challenge. The project combines reversible BTA supramolecular assembly with covalent and dynamic-covalent reactive handles, creating hydrogels that can be reinforced or functionalized after assembly. This postdoctoral project will introduce the living component: engineered cells that trigger or carry out selected post-assembly modifications under defined conditions. The work is deliberately interdisciplinary. Success will require rigorous synthetic-biology design and quantitative microbiology, but also curiosity about soft materials and a willingness to connect cellular function with chemical and mechanical measurements. The position offers the opportunity to shape an emerging direction within an established biomaterials team and to develop an independent profile at the interface of synthetic biology and materials science. Key references Hafeez et al. Advanced Materials 2023, 35, 2301242. https://doi.org/10.1002/adma.202301242 Morgan et al. Journal of the American Chemical Society 2024, 146, 27499–27516. https://doi.org/10.1021/jacs.4c08099 Graham et al. Nature Chemical Biology 2024, 20, 1329–2024. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41589-024-01628-y Tang et al. Nature Chemical Biology 2021, 17, 724–731. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41589-021-00779-6 What you bring We are interested in the combination of expertise, independence, and scientific perspective you would bring. The ideal candidate is an experimental synthetic biologist or microbial engineer who enjoys building and testing systems, quantifying their performance, and collaborating closely with researchers from chemistry and biomaterials. Furthermore, you bring: A PhD, completed by the agreed starting date, in Synthetic Biology, Molecular Microbiology, Biotechnology, Bioengineering, Chemical Biology, Biomaterials, or a closely related field. Strong hands-on experience with microbial culture and genetic engineering, including independent design-build-test cycles, molecular cloning, expression-system optimization, and quantitative validation of cellular outputs. A track record of rigorous experimental design, troubleshooting, data analysis, and scientific communication, demonstrated through peer-reviewed research outputs appropriate to career stage. Experience with inducible gene expression, genetic circuits, protein secretion or surface display, enzyme production, metabolic engineering, or biosensing. Depth in at least one of these areas is more important than experience in all of them. Interest in connecting cell function to material properties and willingness to learn or apply hydrogel characterization methods such as rheology, microscopy, spectroscopy, diffusion or transport measurements, and chemical-conversion assays. The ability to work independently while building productive collaborations across synthetic biology, chemistry, materials science, and cell biology. Experience with engineered living materials, microbial encapsulation, biocontainment, hydrogel systems, enzyme-mediated material modification, or quantitative modelling is considered a strong advantage. A careful approach to biosafety, documentation, reproducibility, and responsible research with engineered microorganisms. Excellent command of scientific English (minimum CEFR level C1), as English is the working language of the group. Availability to start by early 2027 (or earlier). This date can be adjusted for highly qualified applicants. What we offer At Maastricht University, you will work in an international, open, and engaged environment. We offer: A 18-month contract (1,0 FTE) with the possibility of extension. A gross monthly salary between € 3.706 and € 5.760 (based on full-time employment of 38 hours per week). 8% holiday allowance and an 8.3% year-end bonus. 29 vacation days (based on full-time), four additional days off (Carnival Monday and Tuesday, Good Friday, and Liberation Day), and the possibility to accrue up to 12 extra days through compensation hours. Freedom and space to shape your work independently and develop your ideas. A close-knit community of colleagues to collaborate and grow with. A solid pension plan via ABP, company fitness schemes, and access to various university sports facilities. An inspiring work environment in the heart of Europe. About the Faculty of Health, Medicine and Life Sciences (FHML) FHML is committed to health in the broadest sense: from molecule to human, and from healthcare to prevention. We train healthcare professionals and researchers through innovative educational programs and conduct groundbreaking research in health and well-being. As part of Maastricht UMC+ (MUMC+), our international and interdisciplinary community forms a unique collaboration between university and academic hospital, where education, research, and care come together. About MERLN Research Institute MERLN (Institute for Technology-Inspired Regenerative Medicine) within FHML develops new technologies for the repair, regeneration, and in vitro modeling of tissues and organs. Within MERLN, the Department of Instructive Biomaterials Engineering (IBE) uses materials science and engineering to design biomaterials for applications ranging from medical devices and scaffolds for regenerative therapies to predictive in vitro models. By controlling material chemistry, structure, mechanics, and biological functionality, IBE researchers create systems that actively interact with and guide biological processes. The department brings together expertise in polymeric, inorganic, supramolecular, and bioactive materials at the interface of chemistry, engineering, and regenerative medicine. Interested? The deadline for submitting your application is 15 November 2026. Please submit: CV Cover letter explaining your motivation and relevant experience Names and contact details of two referees The selection process consists of two interviews: one conducted online and one on-site. During the on-site interview, candidates will be asked to give a presentation, followed by individual meetings with team members. For more information about the scientific content of the position, contact Dr. Matthew Baker via m.baker@maastrichtuniversity.nl. Apply now via the button below. We look forward to getting to know you! About Maastricht University At Maastricht University, we collaboratively seek solutions to help move the world forward. We do this with 23,300 students and 5,400 employees across 5 regional locations, 6 faculties, and more than 70 research institutes. We encourage you to push boundaries and discover new opportunities for yourself and the world around you. Together, we can find the answers for tomorrow. The vacancy is open to internal and external candidates. In case of equal qualifications, internal candidates will be prioritized. At Maastricht University, we prefer to contact potential candidates directly. We therefore kindly ask that no agencies or intermediaries submit offers or approaches. Maastricht University is committed to promoting and nurturing a diverse and inclusive community. We believe that diversity in our staff and student population contributes to the quality of research and education at UM, and strive to enable this through inclusive policies and innovative projects led by teams of staff and students. We encourage you to apply for this position. Job Type: Academic Faculty/Service Center: Faculty of Health, Medicine and Life Sciences Closing date: 15-11-2026 FTE min: 1,0 FTE max: 1,0 Salary min: €3706,00 Salary max: €5760,00 ID job: 3785 Job Type: Academic Faculty/Service Center: Faculty of Health, Medicine and Life Sciences Closing date: 15-11-2026 FTE min: 1,0 FTE max: 1,0 Salary min: €3706,00 Salary max: €5760,00 ID job: 3785 Apply now » Find similar jobs: Vacatures, All jobs Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab."
    },
